            <ArticleTitle>Pain in general practice. Pain as a cause of patient-doctor contact.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>97-100</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In 1983 26 general practitioners in a Danish provincial town made a week's survey of pain as the main cause of patient-doctor contact during the day time. The population served was 45 000-50 000 persons of all ages. Coexistent pain which was not the cause of actual contact was not recorded. Out of 2 886 contacts of all causes 641 were due to pain (22% or 222/1 000 contacts). Percentages for acute and chronic pain were 61 and 39 respectively. The commonest causes of pain were musculo-skeletal (50%), visceral including cardio-vascular (20%), infectious (15%), and headaches (8%). The overall female: male ratio was 1.5: 1, but with considerable variation within the different pain categories. The ratios for acute and chronic pain were 1.4: 1 and 1.8: 1 respectively. About one hundred contacts were recorded as &quot;problem cases&quot; whose predominant complaints were low back pain, headaches, and visceral pain. Pain--especially chronic pain with a non-malignant cause--is a major problem in general practice. Essentially, pain is a primary health care problem and research in this field should be encouraged.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
